1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,675
A change that raised a batch size was
merged, deployed by CI, the team's automated

2
00:00:09,715 --> 00:00:13,301
pipeline, and now exhausts
memory on the feature workers.

3
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:18,643
Three teammates have pulled it. The CTO
wants to know what exactly you run, and what

4
00:00:18,683 --> 00:00:19,661
nobody must run.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,963
Nobody must run a reset followed by a
forced push. The last two videos explained

6
00:00:25,003 --> 00:00:30,154
why: three clones still name that commit.
What you run is one command that needs no

7
00:00:30,194 --> 00:00:35,406
force, takes the same route as any other
commit, and reaches every clone as a normal

8
00:00:35,446 --> 00:00:40,659
update. And when you understand what that
command computes, you can also explain the

9
00:00:40,699 --> 00:00:45,349
day it stops with a conflict. Hold on
to that day. It arrives in step five.

10
00:00:47,189 --> 00:00:50,712
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:50,752 --> 00:00:55,092
This is the undo for shared history,
the commits other people already have.

12
00:00:55,132 --> 00:00:59,998
In the map from two videos ago, the deciding
question was private or shared, and the

13
00:01:00,038 --> 00:01:02,976
answer "shared" led to one
rule: correct by adding.

14
00:01:03,016 --> 00:01:05,137
git revert is that rule as a command.

15
00:01:05,477 --> 00:01:10,030
Six things today. What a revert is: a
three-way merge with unusual inputs.

16
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:14,002
What it writes inside .git. Three
refusals that surprise people.

17
00:01:14,042 --> 00:01:18,409
How to revert a range, and how to
pack several reverts into one commit.

18
00:01:18,449 --> 00:01:23,436
Why a revert of an old commit can conflict,
with the three index stages as proof.

19
00:01:23,476 --> 00:01:26,912
And what to do when a sequence
of reverts stops halfway.

20
00:01:27,252 --> 00:01:30,964
Reverting a merge commit is a topic
of its own, and it's the next video.

21
00:01:32,804 --> 00:01:34,216
After this video you can:

22
00:01:34,556 --> 00:01:38,795
Describe a revert as a three-way merge
and name its base, ours and theirs.

23
00:01:39,135 --> 00:01:42,322
Revert one commit, a range,
and several commits as one.

24
00:01:42,662 --> 00:01:45,449
Explain why reverting an
old commit can conflict.

25
00:01:45,789 --> 00:01:48,510
Leave a stopped revert
sequence in a known state.

26
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:54,525
In one sentence: git revert <commit> 🟡
CAUTION adds a commit whose change is the

27
00:01:54,565 --> 00:01:57,423
opposite of what <commit>
changed, and removes nothing.

28
00:01:57,763 --> 00:02:02,426
Why does it exist? Because a branch that
other people have can only move forward

29
00:02:02,466 --> 00:02:05,247
without hurting them. A
revert moves it forward.

30
00:02:05,587 --> 00:02:11,279
Precisely. A revert is a three-way merge,
the same machinery as in the merge module,

31
00:02:11,319 --> 00:02:16,941
with unusual inputs. A three-way merge
combines two versions against a common base.

32
00:02:16,981 --> 00:02:21,513
Here the base is the commit being
reverted: on screen, say d51208b.

33
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:26,971
"Ours" is HEAD, 73572b2. "Theirs" is the
parent of the reverted commit, a49e359.

34
00:02:27,311 --> 00:02:30,540
Think it through with the rule
table from the merge module.

35
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:34,805
Between base and theirs, the difference
is the reverted commit's change, read

36
00:02:34,845 --> 00:02:39,237
backwards. Between base and ours, the
difference is everything that has happened

37
00:02:39,277 --> 00:02:43,669
since. The merge applies the backwards
change to the current files, wherever the

38
00:02:43,709 --> 00:02:46,550
lines it touches are still
as that commit left them.

39
00:02:46,890 --> 00:02:52,098
Three consequences follow. Any commit in
the history can be reverted, not only the

40
00:02:52,138 --> 00:02:57,474
last. A revert can conflict. And the result
is an ordinary commit on top of HEAD, so

41
00:02:57,514 --> 00:02:59,394
the branch only moves forward.

42
00:02:59,734 --> 00:03:05,043
Inside .git: one new commit, with the
previous HEAD commit as its only parent and a

43
00:03:05,083 --> 00:03:10,328
generated message. The reflog, Git's local
list of where each ref has been, gets a

44
00:03:10,368 --> 00:03:13,872
line that starts with revert:.
ORIG_HEAD isn't written.

45
00:03:13,912 --> 00:03:18,705
While a revert is stopped at a conflict,
REVERT_HEAD names the commit being

46
00:03:18,745 --> 00:03:22,443
reverted, and .git/sequencer/
holds the steps still to do.

47
00:03:22,783 --> 00:03:27,339
When not to use it? On private history,
where you would rather the mistake never

48
00:03:27,379 --> 00:03:32,164
appeared. There a reset or an amend is
cleaner. And be aware of what a revert isn't:

49
00:03:32,204 --> 00:03:34,749
it reverses one commit,
not everything since.

50
00:03:36,589 --> 00:03:41,106
A picture helps. The textbook's
analogy: a reversing entry in a ledger.

51
00:03:41,146 --> 00:03:46,497
A posted entry is never erased. A second
entry with the opposite sign is posted, and

52
00:03:46,537 --> 00:03:47,653
both stay visible.

53
00:03:47,993 --> 00:03:53,041
It breaks in two places. A ledger reversal
always nets to zero, while a revert is

54
00:03:53,081 --> 00:03:57,878
computed against the files as they're
now, so later changes to the same lines

55
00:03:57,918 --> 00:04:02,590
produce a conflict. And a revert reverses
one commit, not everything since.

56
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:07,466
The first break is the important one. A
revert isn't "go back to how it was before

57
00:04:07,506 --> 00:04:12,041
that commit". It is "apply the opposite of
that commit's change to today's files".

58
00:04:12,081 --> 00:04:16,673
If today's files have moved on in the same
lines, Git has two candidates and no way

59
00:04:16,713 --> 00:04:17,231
to choose.

60
00:04:19,071 --> 00:04:22,773
Before: four commits, with
main on the last, 73572b2.

61
00:04:23,113 --> 00:04:28,190
After git revert d51208b, nothing on
the left was removed or replaced.

62
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:32,794
d51208b is still in the history,
and so is the commit after it.

63
00:04:32,834 --> 00:04:38,934
The branch is one commit longer. The tree
of the new commit, 124de33, is the tree of

64
00:04:38,974 --> 00:04:42,004
73572b2 with the change of d51208b undone.

65
00:04:43,844 --> 00:04:46,520
Step 1: revert a commit
that is not the last.

66
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:51,922
Predict the log afterwards: how many
lines, and is d51208b among them?

67
00:04:51,962 --> 00:04:56,296
And predict the timeout line in
serve.yaml. Say it out loud.

68
00:04:56,336 --> 00:04:57,025
I'll wait.

69
00:05:01,865 --> 00:05:06,350
The log grew by one commit, 124de33,
and d51208b is still in it.

70
00:05:06,390 --> 00:05:12,077
The cache is on again, and the timeout
change of a49e359 is untouched: 5 seconds.

71
00:05:12,417 --> 00:05:14,499
Step 2: inside .git.

72
00:05:14,839 --> 00:05:20,368
One parent, the previous HEAD. A generated
message: "This reverts commit", followed

73
00:05:20,408 --> 00:05:23,858
by the full ID. The reflog
line starts with revert:.

74
00:05:24,198 --> 00:05:28,473
Step 3: three refusals. First,
revert the same commit again.

75
00:05:28,813 --> 00:05:34,421
Nothing to commit, exit status 1. Second,
a habit carried over from git commit.

76
00:05:34,461 --> 00:05:38,281
Quick quiz: what does -m
"some text" do on git revert?

77
00:05:38,321 --> 00:05:42,213
A, it sets the message. B,
Git refuses it. Your answer?

78
00:05:47,053 --> 00:05:50,512
B. "Option mainline expects
a number greater than zero."

79
00:05:50,552 --> 00:05:55,761
On git revert, -m isn't a message but the
parent number for reverting a merge, which

80
00:05:55,801 --> 00:05:58,948
is the next video. Almost
everyone types this once.

81
00:05:58,988 --> 00:06:04,197
The reason for a revert goes into the editor,
which git revert opens when it runs in

82
00:06:04,237 --> 00:06:04,885
a terminal.

83
00:06:05,225 --> 00:06:07,502
Third, the index must match HEAD.

84
00:06:07,842 --> 00:06:13,701
The manual asks for a clean working tree.
Git 2.55 accepts unstaged edits in files

85
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:18,521
that the revert doesn't touch. The
same change, staged, is refused.

86
00:06:18,861 --> 00:06:24,881
Step 4: several commits. A range A..B
excludes A and is reverted newest first, one

87
00:06:24,921 --> 00:06:26,876
revert commit per original.

88
00:06:27,216 --> 00:06:31,860
Two revert commits, the cache one
first because it's the newer original.

89
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:33,616
Timeout 30 again, cache on.

90
00:06:33,956 --> 00:06:38,974
With -n, long form --no-commit, the
inverse changes go into the index and the

91
00:06:39,014 --> 00:06:44,228
working tree, and nothing is committed.
So one commit of your own can carry them

92
00:06:44,268 --> 00:06:44,491
all.

93
00:06:44,831 --> 00:06:48,573
One commit, with a message that says
what the two reverts have in common.

94
00:06:48,913 --> 00:06:53,495
Step 5: a conflict. a49e359
changed the timeout from 30 to 5.

95
00:06:53,535 --> 00:06:58,648
A later commit, fe1b93a, changed it
from 5 to 8. You revert a49e359.

96
00:06:58,688 --> 00:07:04,937
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper: write
base, ours and theirs, with the timeout

97
00:07:04,977 --> 00:07:07,817
value in each. Then say them out loud.

98
00:07:20,157 --> 00:07:25,435
Base 5, ours 8, theirs 30. Both sides
changed the line relative to the base: a

99
00:07:25,475 --> 00:07:30,958
conflict. The revert wants to turn 5 back
into 30, and the line no longer says 5.

100
00:07:30,998 --> 00:07:34,640
That's the day from the start
of the video, explained.

101
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:39,465
Read the marker labels: HEAD above,
and below, "parent of a49e359".

102
00:07:39,505 --> 00:07:44,936
Then the proof. git ls-files -s prints
three stages, the three versions the index

103
00:07:44,976 --> 00:07:50,541
holds during a conflict. The git rev-parse
below it prints the blob IDs of the file

104
00:07:50,581 --> 00:07:56,079
in three commits, in the same order: in
a49e359 itself, in HEAD, and in the parent

105
00:07:56,119 --> 00:08:01,211
of a49e359. They match line for line.
Stage 1, the base, is the commit being

106
00:08:01,251 --> 00:08:05,466
reverted. Stage 3 is its parent.
And REVERT_HEAD names a49e359.

107
00:08:05,806 --> 00:08:10,890
Git can't know whether 8 or 30 is right
today. You decide, stage the result, and

108
00:08:10,930 --> 00:08:11,466
continue.

109
00:08:11,806 --> 00:08:17,053
Step 6: a range that stops halfway.
labs/run ch11/revert-sequence.

110
00:08:17,093 --> 00:08:21,058
A two-step range, and the
second revert conflicts.

111
00:08:24,398 --> 00:08:28,057
When the second revert of a range
conflicts, the first is already committed.

112
00:08:31,397 --> 00:08:37,094
The log shows 407f639, a revert, on
top. git status says "You are currently

113
00:08:37,134 --> 00:08:43,137
reverting commit a49e359". .git/sequencer/
has four files: todo holds the steps

114
00:08:43,177 --> 00:08:48,874
still to do, and head holds the commit
where the sequence started, f6c769e.

115
00:08:49,214 --> 00:08:52,485
Four exits, one per row:
continue, skip, abort and quit.

116
00:08:52,825 --> 00:08:56,726
Predict the top of the log
after --abort. Say it out loud.

117
00:09:01,566 --> 00:09:06,694
Back at f6c769e. The revert commit that
had already been made left the branch.

118
00:09:06,734 --> 00:09:12,127
It's still in the reflog, one line down.
The abort is recorded as a reset, and the

119
00:09:12,167 --> 00:09:13,982
sequencer directory is gone.

120
00:09:14,322 --> 00:09:17,102
From the same stopped state, --quit:

121
00:09:17,442 --> 00:09:18,737
And --skip:

122
00:09:19,077 --> 00:09:23,091
Compare each with its row of the table:
what stayed on the branch, and whether a

123
00:09:23,131 --> 00:09:24,661
conflict is still in the index.

124
00:09:26,501 --> 00:09:28,149
Five mistakes to watch for.

125
00:09:28,489 --> 00:09:32,533
Expecting a revert of an old commit
to restore the file "as it was".

126
00:09:32,573 --> 00:09:37,577
Root cause: a revert merges the inverse
change into today's files, with the reverted

127
00:09:37,617 --> 00:09:41,059
commit as base, so later edits
to the same lines conflict.

128
00:09:41,399 --> 00:09:46,219
Typing git revert -m "reason" <commit>.
Root cause: on git revert, -m is the

129
00:09:46,259 --> 00:09:50,055
mainline parent number for
reverting a merge, not a message.

130
00:09:50,395 --> 00:09:55,479
Thinking a range revert is atomic. Root
cause: a range produces one revert commit

131
00:09:55,519 --> 00:10:00,287
per original, newest first, so a stop
at step two leaves step one committed.

132
00:10:00,627 --> 00:10:05,710
Using --quit to back out of a stopped
sequence. Root cause: --quit forgets the

133
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:11,226
sequence but keeps the reverts already made
and leaves the conflict in the index and

134
00:10:11,266 --> 00:10:14,773
the files; --abort is the
one that returns the branch.

135
00:10:15,113 --> 00:10:20,847
Reverting with staged changes. Root cause:
the index must match HEAD before a revert

136
00:10:20,887 --> 00:10:21,328
starts.

137
00:10:23,168 --> 00:10:28,482
Now, out of the lab. Back to the batch size.
The change was merged, deployed by CI,

138
00:10:28,522 --> 00:10:33,319
and exhausts memory on the feature
workers. Three teammates have pulled it.

139
00:10:33,659 --> 00:10:38,736
git revert <commit> followed by git push
needs no force, takes the same route as any

140
00:10:38,776 --> 00:10:43,548
other commit, a pull request if main
requires one, and reaches every clone as a

141
00:10:43,588 --> 00:10:48,604
normal update. The textbook notes that the
Git FAQ names this as the usual way, and

142
00:10:48,644 --> 00:10:52,990
that the revert manual strongly
recommends that the message explain why.

143
00:10:53,330 --> 00:10:57,163
That last point is where teams
save themselves a second incident.

144
00:10:57,203 --> 00:11:00,202
A generated message says
which commit was reverted.

145
00:11:00,242 --> 00:11:05,088
It doesn't say that the workers ran out of
memory at batch size 64, or under which

146
00:11:05,128 --> 00:11:09,914
conditions the change could come back. Six
weeks later someone will want to raise

147
00:11:09,954 --> 00:11:13,608
the batch size again. The revert
commit is where they'll look.

148
00:11:13,948 --> 00:11:18,254
Lab 8.2 runs this incident,
including the teammate's side.

149
00:11:20,094 --> 00:11:27,000
Your turn. Do Lab 8.2, "Revert a pushed
commit", in lab-manual/m08-undo.md.

150
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:28,533
Predict before you type:

151
00:11:28,873 --> 00:11:33,314
Which commands tell you that the bad commit
is shared, and what must you run before

152
00:11:33,354 --> 00:11:34,070
trusting them?

153
00:11:34,410 --> 00:11:39,602
After the revert, how many commits does
git log show, and what does git status -sb

154
00:11:39,642 --> 00:11:41,133
say about your upstream?

155
00:11:41,473 --> 00:11:46,368
Will the push need force? What will the
teammate's next pull print: a fast-forward,

156
00:11:46,408 --> 00:11:47,736
a merge, or a conflict?

157
00:11:48,076 --> 00:11:53,291
The challenge is Exercise 8.5,
Level 2, "revert a range", in

158
00:11:53,331 --> 00:11:56,182
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

159
00:11:58,022 --> 00:12:03,234
Q180: "Describe git revert as a three-way
merge: what are base, ours and theirs?

160
00:12:03,274 --> 00:12:07,698
Use the answer to explain why
reverting an old commit can conflict."

161
00:12:10,038 --> 00:12:11,366
Answer out loud first.

162
00:12:16,206 --> 00:12:21,132
A strong answer names the three inputs
without hesitation, and says why that choice

163
00:12:21,172 --> 00:12:25,977
of base produces the inverse change. For
the second half it constructs a concrete

164
00:12:26,017 --> 00:12:30,942
case with three values of one line, places
each value in base, ours and theirs, and

165
00:12:30,982 --> 00:12:35,728
reads the conflict off the three-way rule.
If you can also say what the conflict

166
00:12:35,768 --> 00:12:40,693
markers will be labelled, and which index
stage holds which version, you have shown

167
00:12:40,733 --> 00:12:43,445
that the model is
operational and not recited.

168
00:12:45,285 --> 00:12:48,212
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

169
00:12:48,552 --> 00:12:52,937
git revert adds a commit that applies
the opposite of one commit's change, and

170
00:12:52,977 --> 00:12:56,851
removes nothing, so it's the undo
for history that other people have.

171
00:12:57,191 --> 00:13:01,585
It's a three-way merge with the reverted
commit as base, HEAD as ours and the

172
00:13:01,625 --> 00:13:06,423
reverted commit's parent as theirs. It
conflicts when later commits changed the same

173
00:13:06,463 --> 00:13:06,769
lines.

174
00:13:07,109 --> 00:13:12,574
A range is reverted newest first, one
commit per original, and -n lets me combine

175
00:13:12,614 --> 00:13:13,526
them into one.

176
00:13:13,866 --> 00:13:18,538
A stopped sequence has four exits: continue,
skip, abort and quit, and only abort

177
00:13:18,578 --> 00:13:21,389
returns the branch to where
the sequence started.

178
00:13:23,229 --> 00:13:26,066
Read section 11.8 of Chapter 11.

179
00:13:26,406 --> 00:13:32,505
Do Exercise 8.2, Level 1, "revert a
commit that is not the last one", in

180
00:13:32,545 --> 00:13:35,318
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

181
00:13:35,658 --> 00:13:40,679
You can now undo a commit that three people
have pulled, with no force, and explain

182
00:13:40,719 --> 00:13:44,824
the conflict on the day it comes.
Run the lab before the next video.

183
00:13:44,864 --> 00:13:48,178
Next time: reverting a merge,
and the re-merge problem.

184
00:13:48,218 --> 00:13:51,348
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

185
00:13:51,388 --> 00:13:52,811
See you in the next one.
